CORE dfcc-only
main.c
--dfcc main --apply-loop-contracts _ --no-standard-checks
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^\[main.assertion.1\] .* y written through a parameter: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.2\] .* g written as a global: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.3\] .* r assigned the result: FAILURE$
^\[main.assertion.4\] .* z only read: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^\[main\.assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
^\[main\.loop_assigns\.\d+\] .*: FAILURE$
--
The loop assigns inference replaces the bodies of the functions that DFCC does
not instrument (here, with the `__VERIFIER` prefix) by summaries of their writes.
Each loop must still havoc what such a function writes: through a parameter, to
a global, and to the variable the result is assigned to. The writes happen in
the sixth iteration, after the base case, so that the assertions after the loops
fail only if the loops havoc their targets. A function that only reads through
its parameter adds nothing to the write set.
